    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Royal Cemetery of Ur, most of whose royal tombs date to about 2600 BCE, held gold and semi-precious stones brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, and attendants killed to accompany their rulers.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> made his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enheduanna">Enheduanna</a> high priestess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanna">Nanna</a> at Ur, and control of that priesthood remained a symbolic marker of political control over the region for five centuries.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a>, governor of Ur under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu-hengal">Utu-hengal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>, made the city the seat of a new kingdom, built its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> and issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-ur-nammu">Code of Ur-Nammu</a>, one of the oldest law codes known.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Woolley believed the attendants in the royal tombs had gone willingly to their deaths; CT scans of surviving skulls show they were killed by blows to the head, perhaps from the spiked end of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> axe.</p></aside>
    </section>

