    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/meskalamdug"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meskalamdug.jpg" alt="Meskalamdug" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/meskalamdug">Meskalamdug</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_helmet_of_Meskalamdug_in_the_British_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Akieboy, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ur, now Tell el-Muqayyar in Dhi Qar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, stood near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>; the coast has since moved and the mound lies 16 km south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasiriyah">Nasiriyah</a>. Settlement began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubaid-period">Ubaid period</a>, around 3800 BCE by one reckoning, in marshy surroundings where fish, birds, tubers and reeds could feed a town without irrigation, and the canals that cross the site were probably used for transport. A thick layer of clay over the Ubaid levels was read by the excavators of the 1920s as the Flood of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a>, though later carbon dating gave such layers at different Sumerian cities different dates. Proto-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets of about 2900 BCE show writing in use in the Early Dynastic town.</p><p>Ur enters written history as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> in the 26th century BCE. Mesannepada is the first of its kings named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a>, and a cylinder inscribed with his name was found in the Royal Cemetery; five more kings followed before the List says the kingship was taken by Awan. The List may omit an earlier line, the so-called Kalam dynasty, to which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meskalamdug">Meskalamdug</a> and Akalamdug perhaps belonged. The city's patron was the moon god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanna">Nanna</a>, Sin in Akkadian, and its name derives from UNUGKI, 'the abode of Nanna'. The occupied area grew from about 15 hectares in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jemdet-nasr-period">Jemdet Nasr period</a> to 90 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/standard-of-ur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/standard-of-ur.jpg" alt="Standard of Ur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/standard-of-ur">Standard of Ur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denis_Bourez_-_British_Museum,_London_(8747049029)_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Denis Bourez from France, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The wealth of Early Dynastic Ur is plainest in the Royal Cemetery. Its tombs of the Early Dynastic IIIa period held luxury goods of precious metals and semi-precious stones brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, evidence of the city's commercial reach in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a>. Among about 1,850 burials Woolley counted sixteen or seventeen royal tombs; the unlooted tomb of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puabi">Puabi</a>, known from a cylinder seal, yielded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/standard-of-ur">Standard of Ur</a>, and the Lyres of Ur, bull-shaped instruments with eleven strings, came to light in 1928. The people buried with the dead bore the skeletal marks of long manual labour.</p><p>Ur stayed a trading city long after its kings. In the Isin-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> period, when the occupied area reached 140 hectares, the merchant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ea-nasir">Ea-nasir</a> dealt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a>, shipping ingots that originated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magan">Magan</a> by water to Ur for resale and probably supplying the palace; he was active in the 11th and 19th regnal years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rim-sin-i">Rim-Sin I</a> of Larsa. The Complaint tablet to Ea-nasir, written c. 1750 BCE in Akkadian and found by Woolley in what is thought to have been his house at 'Number 1 Old Street', carries the grievance of a customer, Nanni, who had paid in advance and whose servant was offered poor ingots with the equivalent of 'take it or leave it'; other letters in the house complain of copper never delivered. Guinness World Records lists it as the oldest written customer complaint.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/eannatum"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eannatum.jpg" alt="Eannatum" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/eannatum">Eannatum</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P1130735_Louvre_st%C3%A8le_des_Vautours_rwk.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ur's wars are known mostly from its defeats and from the arms buried with its dead. In the 'King's Grave', PG 789, lay 63 attendants equipped with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> helmets and swords, thought to be a ruler's army buried with him; in the Great Death Pit, PG 1237, 74 bodies were found, 68 of them women, the men with weapons and whetstones. Early Dynastic Ur was itself conquered: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eannatum">Eannatum of Lagash</a> in the 25th century BCE, Lugalkinishedudu of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> around 2400 BCE and Lugalzagesi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> in the 24th century all held the city.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a>'s conquest met resistance. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rimush">Rimush</a> had to put down a revolt led by a local ruler of Ur; an inscription records 8,040 men killed, 5,460 taken prisoner and the leaders of the rebellion captured, and Rimush dedicated vessels seized in his Elamite campaign to the gods at Ur. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> set up triumphal inscriptions in the city that scribal students copied for centuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> fell in the 22nd century BCE to internal strain and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutians">Gutians</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a>, and it is not known whether a Gutian king or a local governor then ruled Ur. In the 21st century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu-hengal">Utu-hengal</a> of Uruk drove the Gutians out of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>; on his death his governor at Ur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a>, moved the kingship there. The Third Dynasty's own end came by arms, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a> took the city and carried off King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibbi-sin">Ibbi-Sin</a>, the event mourned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lament-for-ur">Lament for Ur</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/code-of-ur-nammu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/code-of-ur-nammu.jpg" alt="Code of Ur-Nammu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/code-of-ur-nammu">Code of Ur-Nammu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ur_Nammu_code_Istanbul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Istanbul Archaeology Museums, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kingship at Ur was bound to the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanna">Nanna</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> names only thirteen rulers in three dynasties of Ur, and the once-supposed second dynasty may never have existed. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> took the city he appointed his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enheduanna">Enheduanna</a> high priestess of Nanna, an act that historians such as Marc van de Mieroop read as a way of legitimising his rule over southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>; control of that priesthood remained a symbolic marker of political control over the region for five centuries. The office survived every change of master: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> appointed Enmenana, Ur-Bau of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> installed Enanepada and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a> appointed Ennirgalana.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> Ur served as the hub of southern Babylonia, and the Akkadians imposed their language on the Sumerian-speaking population, adapting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> to its grammar. Ur-Nammu, who ruled c. 2047–2030 BCE by one chronology and c. 2112–2094 BCE by another, built temples including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat-of-ur">Ziggurat of Ur</a>, improved agriculture by irrigation and issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-ur-nammu">Code of Ur-Nammu</a>, which precedes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi</a> by 300 years. He and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shulgi">Shulgi</a> were deified in their lifetimes; Shulgi, who ruled at least 42 years, reshaped the realm into a highly centralised bureaucratic state. The Akiti festival of Nanna ran each year from the god's temple in Ur to Ga'esh, perhaps the site of Tell Sakhariya, a day's journey away by canal.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/enheduanna"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enheduanna.jpg" alt="Enheduanna" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/enheduanna">Enheduanna</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enheduanna,_daughter_of_Sargon_of_Akkad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mefman00, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ur was one of the religious and literary centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enheduanna">Enheduanna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon</a>'s daughter and high priestess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanna">Nanna</a>, composed hymns such as The Exaltation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Inanna</a> and a Temple Hymn to the Karzida sanctuary at Ga'esh, and is a candidate for the first identifiable author in world history, though Assyriologists are unsure whether a scribe wrote on her behalf. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Ur III</a> period hymns praising rulers became a popular genre, and a poem describes the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a> and his journey to the underworld; at that period's end the Lament for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> and Ur and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lament-for-ur">Lament for Ur</a> recorded the city's fall. By one estimate Ur was the largest city in the world from c. 2030 to 1980 BCE, with about 65,000 people.</p><p>The city's name travelled further than its goods. Ur is possibly the 'Ur of the Chaldees' that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a> names as the birthplace of Abraham, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanliurfa">Şanlıurfa</a>, Urkesh, Urartu and Kutha have also been proposed, and the Chaldeans did not settle nearby until about 850 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-francis">Pope Francis</a> visited the site in March 2021. The Royal Cemetery made headlines in the 1920s and drew visitors by rail to 'Ur Junction'; Agatha Christie met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-mallowan">Max Mallowan</a>, Woolley's assistant, there. The treasures are now divided between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penn-museum">Penn Museum</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> Museum, and the partly restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a>, built by Ur-Nammu and repaired by Ishme-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurigalzu-i">Kurigalzu I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a>, remains the best-preserved landmark of the site.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/kesh-temple-hymn">Kesh Temple Hymn<span>c. 2600 BCE</span></a><a href="/songs/lament-for-ur">Lament for Ur<span>c. 2000 BCE</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibbi-sin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibbi-sin.jpg" alt="Ibbi-Sin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ibbi-sin">Ibbi-Sin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ibbi-Sin_enthroned.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a>'s independence ended not with a sack but with a promotion: when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a> moved the kingship to Ur, the city became the capital of an empire, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a> ruled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shulgi">Shulgi</a>, Amar-Sin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shu-sin">Shu-Sin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibbi-sin">Ibbi-Sin</a>. In Ibbi-Sin's 24th regnal year, around 1940 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a> took Ur and carried off its king; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lament-for-ur">Lament for Ur</a> commemorates the disaster, and archaeology suggests the city was severely damaged.</p><p>What followed was a long afterlife. Ur stayed important under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isin">Isin</a>, then fell to the Elamite kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a>, who rebuilt much, added to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> and made Ur their second capital; it was in Larsa's time that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ea-nasir">Ea-nasir</a> traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a> and received the Complaint tablet to Ea-nasir, c. 1750 BCE. In the 19th century BCE, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rim-sin-i">Rim-Sin I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> conquered the city, and in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsu-iluna">Samsu-iluna</a> it was abandoned. It later belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sealand-dynasty">Sealand Dynasty</a>, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassites">Kassites</a> from the 16th century BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurigalzu-i">Kurigalzu I</a> rebuilt the temple of Ningal, at times to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-assyrian-empire">Middle Assyrian Empire</a>, and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a> from the 10th to the late 7th century BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> of Babylon built there in the 6th century BCE, but after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> fell to the Achaemenid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> around 530 BCE Ur declined, and by the early 5th century BCE it was uninhabited, perhaps through drought, shifting rivers and the silting of the Gulf outlet.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Ur, a Sumerian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> near the ancient mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> and seat of the moon god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanna">Nanna</a>, rose to great wealth in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a>, passed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, and in c. 2112 BCE became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur-nammu">Ur-Nammu</a>'s Third Dynasty, whose fall to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lament-for-ur">Lament for Ur</a> mourns.</em></p>

